    description: "The counterintuitive finding: mentally 'putting yourself in another's shoes' (perspective-taking) does not make you more accurate about their thoughts and feelings. Across 25 experiments, Eyal, Steffel & Epley (2018) found not one showed a benefit of instructed perspective-taking on accuracy, and it sometimes made judgments worse by increasing confidence. What did improve accuracy was perspective-GETTING: actually asking the person and listening to their answer. The practical skill is inquiry, not imagination.",
