    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "months", durability: "sustained", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["shock resilience", "avoiding high-cost debt spirals", "reduced financial stress", "decision-making under scarcity"],
    description: "A modest liquid buffer absorbs income and expense shocks before they cascade into high-cost debt or missed necessities. Lusardi, Schneider & Tufano documented that a large share of households could not cope with a $2,000 shock in 30 days - a strong predictor of hardship. Urban Institute analysis found households that hit even ~$2,400 in liquid savings were markedly less likely to suffer material hardship up to three years later.",
    callout: "Most of this evidence is observational, so reverse causation is real - stable, higher-income households both save more and face fewer shocks. The buffer helps, but it is a symptom as much as a cause of financial stability; effect sizes shrink with income controls.",
