    description: "Fees look trivial as a one-year percentage but compound into a large slice of terminal wealth, and most of what active management charges buys no net outperformance. Carhart (1997) shows expense ratios and turnover reduce mutual-fund net returns roughly one-for-one. French (2008) estimates the typical investor could raise their return ~0.67% per year simply by switching from active to passive. Arithmetic makes the stakes concrete: a 1% higher annual fee compounds to roughly a quarter less wealth over a 30-year horizon.",
