    description: "People believe they understand how complex things work — zippers, bicycles, policies — in far more mechanistic detail than they actually do, until asked to produce a step-by-step explanation, at which point self-rated understanding drops sharply. Rozenblit and Keil showed the effect is specific to explanatory/causal knowledge (not facts or procedures) and arises from confusing knowing a label with grasping a mechanism. Fernbach and colleagues later showed that asking people to explain a policy mechanistically reduced their confidence and political extremity.",
