    degrades: ["Willingness to sustain effortful thinking", "Depth of engagement with material", "Cumulative achievement"],
    description: "Need for cognition is the disposition to seek out and enjoy effortful thinking; people low in it avoid cognitively demanding tasks and engage more superficially. A large meta-analysis (Liu & Nesbit, 136 effect sizes, N = 53,258) found a small but reliable positive association with academic achievement, r = 0.20, stronger in older/postsecondary students. Low need for cognition thus tilts a learner toward avoidance of exactly the deep processing that builds durable understanding.",
    callout: "The effect is small and correlational, and need for cognition overlaps with conscientiousness and prior ability, so its unique contribution is modest — do not overstate it as a primary driver. It is a disposition that can be nudged by interest and task design, not a hard limit.",
