    id: "hedonic-adaptation-entrapment", section: "695", title: "Hedonic Adaptation Entrapment → The Treadmill", subtitle: "Degrades: Existential, Emotional, Intrapersonal capacity",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    weakness: { threat: 4, weakLines: ["Existential", "Emotional", "Intrapersonal"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "years", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Durable satisfaction from achievement", "Freedom from compulsive acquisition", "Present-moment contentment"],
    description: "People habituate to improved circumstances — raises, purchases, status — so gains in happiness fade toward a baseline. When someone stakes their well-being strategy on the next acquisition or achievement, they get trapped on a 'hedonic treadmill', chronically striving without lasting gain. Diener, Lucas, and Scollon's revision shows adaptation is real but incomplete and heterogeneous: set points are not neutral, can shift with major life events (e.g., disability, unemployment leave lasting marks), and people differ in how fully they adapt.",
    callout: "Adaptation is largely an adaptive feature, not a defect — the 'weakness' is the strategy of chasing circumstance change for happiness; classic strong 'return to baseline' claims are overstated, and the treadmill construct is theoretical/observational rather than experimentally proven.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Diener, E., Lucas, R. E., & Scollon, C. N. (2006). Beyond the hedonic treadmill: Revising the adaptation theory of well-being. American Psychologist, 61(4), 305–314.", note: "Landmark review: adaptation is incomplete and variable; set points can change and some events (disability, widowhood, unemployment) leave lasting well-being decrements. Limitation: synthesis/theory, not a single effect size. [Moderate — authoritative review]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.61.4.305", kind: "doi" },
    ],
