    description: "Meditation is not one thing: focused-attention (FA) practice repeatedly returns attention to a single object (breath, mantra), training top-down concentration, while open-monitoring (OM) rests in non-reactive awareness of whatever arises, training meta-awareness and flexibility. The Lutz et al. (2008) framework and later EEG reviews show partially distinct neural signatures (FA: frontal-midline theta / dorsal-attention engagement; OM: distributed dynamics, gamma synchrony). Matching style to goal — FA to steady a scattered mind, OM to loosen rumination — is a defensible design principle, but the outcome differences between styles are far softer than the mechanistic story implies.",
    callout: "EEG findings are heterogeneous and cross-sectional (long-term meditators differ from novices for many reasons); head-to-head clinical trials rarely show one style clearly beating the other, so treat the FA/OM split as a useful teaching frame, not a proven dose-response lever.",
