    description: "The illusion of transparency is the tendency to overestimate how much others can read our internal states — our nervousness, disgust, or a lie 'leaking out.' Gilovich, Savitsky, & Medvec (1998) showed liars overestimated how detectable their lies were and speakers overestimated how visible their nervousness was, because we anchor on our own strong inner experience and under-correct for others' limited access. It undermines communication (assuming 'they must know what I mean/feel') and worsens performance anxiety.",
