    description: "Under Leventhal's common-sense model, patients' beliefs about their illness — its consequences, controllability, timeline, and emotional weight — shape coping and outcomes. Meta-analyses using the (Brief) IPQ show consistent correlations (roughly r = .25–.49) between perceptions of serious consequences, strong illness identity, and emotional representations and worse depression, anxiety, disability, and quality of life; stronger perceived personal control tracks with better outcomes. These beliefs are modifiable and predict recovery across cardiac, diabetes, and pain populations.",
