    id: "moral-hypocrisy", section: "1011", title: "Moral Hypocrisy → Appearing Moral Without Being So", subtitle: "Degrades: Moral, Intrapersonal, Meta-Cognitive",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Moral", "Intrapersonal"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["matching private behavior to publicly professed values", "comparing one's own conduct to one's own standards", "resisting the pull to look good rather than be good"],
    description: "Moral hypocrisy is the motivation to APPEAR moral while avoiding the cost of actually being moral. In Batson's coin-flip studies, most people given a private, riggable coin to assign a good vs. bad task assigned the good one to themselves far more than chance — yet rated their unfair choice as 'moral.' The trick is avoidance of comparing one's behavior to the standard, not misperceiving the behavior itself. This lets people exploit others while sincerely defending their own virtue, corroding trust when the gap is exposed.",
    callout: "Effects come from small-sample lab paradigms (coin flips, task assignment) from one primary research group; while conceptually influential, the specific quantitative estimates are not backed by large multi-lab replications, so read it as a well-demonstrated phenomenon of uncertain population magnitude.",
