    description: "Leaders who over-rely on specific, aggressive stretch goals (especially tied to rewards) trigger predictable side effects. Experimental work shows specific high goals increase cheating and misreporting, particularly when people fall just short. The 'Goals Gone Wild' review catalogs how goal-setting can narrow focus to the point of neglecting unspecified but vital tasks, promote risk-taking and unethical shortcuts, inhibit learning, and corrode cooperation and intrinsic motivation.",
    callout: "Contested: goal-setting theory (Locke & Latham) has decades of strong evidence that specific hard goals raise performance — the critique is about overprescription and poorly designed goals, and some 'goals gone wild' claims are still debated and being replicated. The harm is real but conditional on goal design and incentives.",
