    id: "design-fixation-anchor-first-idea", section: "1042", title: "Design Fixation → Anchoring on the First (or Only) Example You Saw", subtitle: "Degrades: Creative, Strategic",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    weakness: { threat: 6, weakLines: ["Creative", "Strategic"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Exploring a wide concept space", "Escaping an anchor / first solution", "Generating structurally novel alternatives"],
    description: "Design fixation is blind adherence to a starting idea or a provided example that narrows the space of concepts explored. In Jansson & Smith's classic study, engineers and students shown a flawed example design reproduced its features — including the flaw they were implicitly warned against — far more than a no-example control group. Fixation makes the first idea the enemy of the best idea, and it persists even when people know the anchor is imperfect.",
