    description: "Derber's ethnographic work on informal conversation identified the 'shift response' — repeatedly steering talk back to oneself — as the engine of conversational narcissism, versus the 'support response' that keeps attention on the partner. Experimental work converges: people who ask more questions, especially follow-up questions, are better liked, an effect mediated by perceived responsiveness (across get-acquainted dyads and a speed-dating field study). Monologuing and failing to reciprocate self-disclosure therefore suppress liking and depth precisely because they signal low responsiveness.",
