    id: "drowning-anoxic-brain-injury", section: "1157", title: "Drowning Survival & Anoxic Brain Injury", subtitle: "Degrades: memory & cognition, motor function, independence",
    evidenceTag: "Emerging",
    harm: { severity: 5, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "lasting" },
    degrades: ["Memory and cognition", "Motor function", "Independent daily functioning"],
    description: "Non-fatal drowning can leave survivors with hypoxic-ischemic (anoxic) brain injury whose severity tracks submersion time and resuscitation quality. The most vulnerable regions — hippocampus, basal ganglia, insular cortex — produce persistent memory, cognitive, and motor deficits, and after a significant hypoxic insult full recovery is not the norm. Coma length and functional status on admission are strong predictors of long-term outcome. Onset is immediate and severe injury is typically lasting.",
    callout: "Evidence is largely observational and prognostic; outcomes are highly bimodal (near-full recovery vs. severe disability) so group averages are misleading, and much of the pediatric literature comes from small case series.",
