    harm: { severity: 4, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Cognition and attention", "Hearing", "Emotional regulation", "Sleep"],
    description: "Explosive-blast exposure is the signature injury of recent military conflicts, producing a distinctive polytrauma-plus-mild-TBI picture. A systematic review of 106 studies (37,515 participants; Frontiers in Neurology, 2020) found the most common sequelae are hearing disturbance and headache, with frequent comorbid PTSD, depression, anxiety, sleep, attention, and cognitive disorders. An estimated 10–20% have post-concussive symptoms persisting beyond three months, and blast mTBI often goes undiagnosed amid more visible injuries. Onset is immediate; recovery is partial.",
