    degrades: ["Social confidence and interpersonal ease", "Body image", "Emotional wellbeing"],
    description: "Visible facial scarring and disfigurement carry a persistent affective and social burden. A meta-analysis of 21 studies (Gibson et al., 2018; 2,394 participants) found pooled anxiety of ~26% and depression of ~21% among people with facial scars, and a population cohort of 358,158 patients confirmed elevated anxiety and depression versus matched controls. Crucially, distress is predicted more by subjective self-perception and social interactions than by objective scar severity. Onset is immediate; the psychological burden is often lasting.",
