    degrades: ["Neck and physical function", "Work and occupational capacity", "Mood", "Sleep"],
    description: "Most acute whiplash injuries improve, but recovery levels off after about three months and roughly half of patients transition to chronic neck pain and disability, with signs of central nervous-system pain amplification. A systematic review and meta-analysis (Kamper et al., 2008) established this plateau pattern; the strongest predictors of poor recovery are high initial pain/disability, post-traumatic stress symptoms, pain catastrophizing, and low recovery expectations. Onset is immediate but chronicity develops over months; outcomes are partially reversible.",
