    id: "pesticide-exposure-parkinsons", section: "1203", title: "Pesticide Exposure → Parkinson's Disease", subtitle: "Degrades: Motor control, Cognitive function, Physical independence",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    harm: { severity: 4, onset: "years", reversibility: "lasting" },
    degrades: ["Motor control and coordination", "Cognitive function", "Physical independence"],
    description: "Occupational pesticide exposure (farmers, plantation and applicator workers) is a well-replicated Parkinson's-disease risk factor. Meta-analyses report weighted relative risks around 1.28–1.67 for 'any pesticide,' rising to ~2.5 in the best-designed studies with neurologist-confirmed diagnoses, and ~2.05 for banana/sugarcane/pineapple plantation workers. Specific agents (paraquat, rotenone, organochlorines) carry the strongest mechanistic support.",
